$D$ in the upper half plane bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281647 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec31a85b-303d-5111-a311-f0cb89d6a30f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281647 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}=8\\pi .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{8\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot 64\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =4M.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281844 #edb/ref 1080 "A hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(-1,2,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281844 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281844 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10cde161-28f6-55e5-986b-ad9855da614a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281863 #edb/ref 1080 "A hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(1,-2,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281863 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281863 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "122b7f1a-d06c-5752-b881-d3aaf0a1f656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282000 #edb/ref 1080 "A parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $x$-axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282000 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282000 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "232f26c9-79a9-5402-b0f7-63e58ec7851c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282313 #edb/ref 1080 "An ellipsoid centered at $(1,2,-3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282313 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282313 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49873a52-1e06-5d10-9048-c31a56b3f6fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282438 #edb/ref 1080 "(24,26)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282438 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282438 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "580c3843-6361-5a93-8e93-aa4eecc3f021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282508 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,5,2)$ and $(\\sqrt{3},32,8)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282508 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282508 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "60ad0b26-32cf-5d6f-995f-5d31f9a2fbab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282606 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})" #edb/ref 17