1075 #edb/ref 17592186282313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284382 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284382 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284382 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284382 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd434c9b-d384-57dd-8332-85e7b5ec2000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284536 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t}\\,,\\,{t}^{3}+5\\,,\\,2t+2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc3f1136-302e-5c6a-a6a1-4ab2d796baa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284536 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,{t}^{3}+5,\\,2t+2⟩ & =c\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,{t}^{3}+5,\\,2t+2⟩ & =⟨\\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3c{t}^{2},\\,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t}=\\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ {t}^{3}+5=3c{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t+2=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2t.$ Substituting into the third equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+2 & =2\\cdot (2t) \\\\ 2t+2 & =4t \\\\ 2t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the second equation ${t}^{3}+5=3c{t}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$ These values also satisfy the second equation. \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\sqrt{1}\\,,\\,{1}^{3}+5\\,,\\,2(1)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,6,\\,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,6,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302742 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302742 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302742 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e27b405-c7c3-5f62-a8f6-6a64cf338f01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303061 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303061 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303061 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "63f3178a-b9c6-5d7e-b736-cc9281b3ce05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303336 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303336 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303336 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d3afe43-bab1-5b9d-9b78-d4bcade00070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303825 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303825 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303825 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dbcca6c4-65bc-5bc2-8b5d-99e7b4b6dea5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303993 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303993 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303993 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f4ca7eaa-ce56-541a-ab75-04508ebe8595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186302742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186303061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186303336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186303825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186303993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304301 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304301 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304301 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186303061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304301 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "51d2ea82-7e8d-541f-94a3-049caf6d6179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1069 "q-330308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed465271-2eba-5278-8938-505251fa9eb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305211 #edb/ref 1080 "unstable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305211 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305211 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0688d970-6dca-56cd-95c0-d86170f24097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305327 #edb/ref 1080 "unstable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305327 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305327 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14748218-cd47-52ed-aed5-7c5ff8daa963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305343 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{5}{18}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305343 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305343 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "161d56e1-91dc-549e-9bfa-2ed40eaef4a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305560 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305560 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218